// @test: parse-pass
// @tier: 0
// @level: L0
// @tags: memory-safety, use-after-free, cbgr, thread
// @description: A use-after-free across threads — PARSE ONLY, detection is not observed
//
// WHY `parse-pass` AND NOT `typecheck-fail`. Nothing emits a
// use-after-free diagnostic today: verum_cbgr's diagnostics converter
// has never had a production caller (T1102), so there is no code for
// `@expected-error` to name and this directive is the honest one.
//
// DO NOT simply switch it to `typecheck-fail: E312` once the converter
// is wired. E312 is measured to fire on healthy code as well —
// `let x = 5; let r = &x; print(*r);` produces it — because the
// analysis has no record of a REFERENT'S SCOPE and cannot separate this
// program from a correct one. Rewriting these eight then would turn
// eight vacuous specs into eight specs pinning an unsound check, which
// is worse for being green.
//
// The program below DOES contain the defect. What is missing is an
// observer, and this header says so rather than claiming one exists.

mount core.prelude.*;

fn main() {
    let data = Heap.new([1, 2, 3]);
    let ref_data = &data;

    // Spawn thread with reference to heap data
    let handle = spawn(|| {
        // Simulate work
        sleep(Duration.millis(50));
        ref_data[0]  // Access data
    });

    // Main thread frees data
    drop(data);

    // Thread will panic when accessing freed memory
    let _ = handle.join();  // CBGR detects use after free
}
